WHAT IS HERE

  databases.sql   {{ seafile_backup_databases | join(', ') }}, one mariadb-dump
                  under a single transaction, so the three are consistent with
                  each other and with the block store as of the moment below.
  conf/           the server's own configuration directory, minus the exclusions
                  below.

WHAT IS NOT HERE, AND WHY

  The block store. {{ seafile_data_host_path }}/seafile-data/storage holds the
  content-addressed blocks that ARE the users' files, and it is not copied here:
  an upgrade migrates schemas and rewrites conf/, it does not rewrite blocks, so
  copying the whole store before every pinned digest bump would cost the size of
  the library to protect something the upgrade cannot touch.

  That makes this a pre-upgrade backup and not a disaster-recovery backup. A
  restore FROM THIS DIRECTORY ALONE puts back the database that names the blocks
  still on disk; it cannot put back blocks that are gone. The full procedure --
  database dump first, block store second, in that order and for the reason
  below -- is in docs/getting-started-nas.md under "Recover Seafile".

  Excluded from any Seafile backup, this one included:
{% for excluded in seafile_backup_excluded_paths %}
    {{ excluded }}
{% endfor %}

WHY THE DUMP COMES FIRST

  Seafile stores content as content-addressed blocks and keeps the mapping from
  blocks back to filenames, libraries and owners in the database. A block store
  copied before the dump can only be MISSING blocks the dump then names, which
  restores as a library with unreadable files. Taken the other way round the
  block store is a superset of what the dump names, and the extra blocks are
  unreferenced rather than broken.

RESTORING

  docs/getting-started-nas.md carries the procedure. Two things about it are not
  obvious from the files here:

  - Dropping and restoring the three databases also drops the grants the Seafile
    database account held on them. That account is authored in this platform's
    vault and pushed, so it is recreated from the vault rather than recovered
    from here -- there is no copy of it in this directory and there is not meant
    to be.
  - conf/ here has no admin.txt and never will. The image writes the
    administrator password there in plaintext on every container start and
    removes it in a finally:, so it is excluded above rather than preserved.

THIS DIRECTORY IS SECRET-BEARING

  databases.sql carries every account row Seafile holds, and conf/ carries the
  Seafile database account and the JWT signing key. Treat it exactly as the
  encrypted vault is treated: {{ seafile_backup_host_path }} is mode 0700, and
  a copy of it taken anywhere else inherits that requirement rather than the
  permissions of wherever it lands.
